.Dd October 7, 2026
.Dt FUGUSEED-LAST 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fuguseed-last
.Nd print the check word of 11 seed words and two dice
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads seed words 1 to 11 and the YELLOW and BLUE faces of word 12 from
standard input.
It prints the check word: the one word of the BLUE row of word 12 that
makes the checksum of the 12 words valid.
The program takes no option and no argument.
.Pp
The words and the faces enter on standard input only.
No argument, no file name, and no environment variable carries a word
or a face.
The program opens no file, it starts no other program, and it reads no
environment variable.
The three standard streams are its one contact with the computer.
.Pp
.Nm
sees the seed words.
Run it on an air-gapped computer only.
.Ss The input
Standard input holds two lines.
The first line holds words 1 to 11, with a space between two words.
Each word must be a word of the English word list of BIP39.
.Pp
The second line holds the YELLOW face and the BLUE face of word 12,
with a space between the two faces.
The YELLOW face is a number from 1 to 8, and the BLUE face is a number
from 1 to 16.
Write each face with no sign and no leading zero.
The faces are a part of the secret: they give 7 bits of word 12.
.Ss The result
Standard output holds the check word on one line.
The YELLOW face gives the block, and the BLUE face gives the row of
word 12.
The RED column gives the 4 bits of the checksum, so one word of that
row makes the checksum valid.
The program computes that word.
.Pp
The check word carries no entropy: it is a function of the other
bits.
The YELLOW block and the BLUE row stay the throw of the dice.
.Ss The next step
Write the check word as word 12 on the paper.
Then start
.Xr fuguseed-qr 1 ,
and type the 12 words.
.Ss The check of the file
The release publishes
.Nm
as one file, beside the tarballs.
The signed SHA256 manifest of the release names that file.
Compare the digest of the file with the manifest on the air-gapped
computer, before the first run:
.Pp
.Dl $ sha256 fuguseed-last
.Pp
The two digests must agree.
Do not run a file with a different digest.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
.Nm
printed the check word.
.It 1
The first line did not hold 11 words of the list, or the second line
did not hold two correct faces.
.Nm
printed one line to standard error.
That line names the word position, the count, or the die, never a word
or a face.
.It 2
The command line held an argument.
.Nm
printed one usage line to standard error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start the program on the air-gapped computer:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguseed-last
.Ed
.Pp
The program waits for the two lines on standard input.
Type words 1 to 11 on the first line, and the two faces on the second
line.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fuguseed-qr 1 ,
.Xr fuguseed-words 1 ,
.Xr fuguseed 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The word list and the checksum come from BIP39, the mnemonic code for
the generation of a deterministic key.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
The check word makes the checksum valid for any 11 words of the list,
so the program cannot find a wrong word in words 1 to 11.
Compare each word with the sheet before you type it.
When the paper holds the same wrong word,
.Xr fuguseed-qr 1
cannot find it either: the 12 words have a valid checksum.
